### Quicker-check artifacts — expensive-to-recreate outputs
The reproducibility bundle lets someone re-run an experiment; also save the outputs that are slow to
recreate so a reviewer, a fresh agent, or the verifier on a long run can re-check a result quickly
without rebuilding the pipeline: trained model weights/checkpoints, generated datasets, computed
operators/dictionaries, fitted-parameter files. Save under `results/data/` (or a campaign-appropriate
directory), keyed by the experiment label, and record their location in the experiment's
`results/logbook.md` entry.

### Additional visualization — noteworthy experiments
When an experiment is a new current-best or otherwise noteworthy, also produce the heavier / explanatory
visualizations: {{PROMOTED_BEST_ARTIFACTS}}
<!-- Extra figures, montages/animations, etc. Keep the artifacts of each successive best so the
     progression is inspectable. -->

### Retention plan — what is kept forever vs regenerated on demand
Disk is a real constraint on a long loop, and a campaign that fills its volume overnight corrupts the
save-set it was in the middle of writing. The driver enforces the floor: it refuses to launch, and
halts at the loop top, below ~3 experiments of headroom. What to keep is a plan you make; the
per-experiment cost and the floor it is measured against are in `campaign.md` §Caps.

Never droppable — the permanent record. `CLAUDE.md` HARD GATE 4 covers the first four in code;
deleting any of the rest costs the record without tripping a gate, so nothing will catch it: the
scoreboard row · `results/result_<NN>.json` · `results/checks_<NN>.json`
(deleting it makes the run indistinguishable from one that bypassed the runner) · the basic figure · the
reproducibility bundle in `results/snapshots/exp<NN>_<slug>/` · the logbook/JOURNAL narrative ·
verdicts. These are small by construction (text, one figure, a code copy), so a retention plan never
needs to touch them.

Negotiable — bulk artifacts (the quicker-check set above: model weights/checkpoints, generated
datasets, computed operators, raw field/trace arrays). These may be dropped only when regeneration is
deterministic and recorded: the snapshot pins the exact config + code, and the manifest pins the seed
and command line, so re-running the snapshot reproduces the artifact bit-for-bit. Rules:
- Record the decision before the loop runs, not after the disk fills: for each bulk artifact, either
  keep or regenerate-on-demand, with the regeneration command in that experiment's `logbook.md`
  entry.
- Keep the current-best's bulk artifacts (and the runner-up's): they are re-checked most often, and a
  verifier waiting on a re-solve is the cost this rule exists to avoid.
- Never drop what cannot be regenerated deterministically. Anything touching hardware, external
  services, wall-clock, or an unrecorded seed is a one-shot measurement, not a cache.
- Dropping is deletion of data: it needs the user's confirmation, it is logged in the logbook, and it
  never happens silently mid-run.
